# CSS Flexbox vs CSS Grid

Both Flexbox and CSS Grid are modern CSS layout models, but they are built for fundamentally different use cases.

---

## Core Difference: 1D vs 2D Layouts

| Feature | CSS Flexbox | CSS Grid |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Dimension** | **1-Dimensional** (Row *OR* Column) | **2-Dimensional** (Rows *AND* Columns simultaneously) |
| **Approach** | **Content-first:** Elements determine their space along an axis. | **Layout-first:** A predefined container grid positions child elements. |
| **Alignment** | Perfect for distributing space along a single row/column. | Perfect for complex dashboard layouts, photo galleries, and page structures. |
| **Overlap** | Difficult to overlap elements cleanly. | Native overlapping support via grid lines and named areas. |

---

## Code Example:

```css
/* Flexbox: 1D Navbar */
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
}

/* Grid: 2D Dashboard */
.dashboard-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr 300px;
  grid-template-rows: 60px 1fr 40px;
  grid-template-areas:
    "sidebar header header"
    "sidebar main   aside"
    "sidebar footer footer";
  gap: 16px;
}
```

> **Rule of Thumb:** Use **Flexbox** for micro-components (buttons, navbars, badges) and **Grid** for overall page layout, tables, and complex two-dimensional matrices.
